[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router4
diagnosis_text:
- Affected component: Dynamic routing on the user LAN. FRR/RIP is enabled on the 192.168.200.0/24 access segment (your users’ VLAN) at the gateway.
- Mechanism: With RIP running on the client-facing interface, router4 multicasts RIPv2 updates (UDP/520 to 224.0.0.9) onto the access LAN every ~30 seconds. On small L2 bridges (switch5/switch6), these periodic control bursts can trigger brief CPU/forwarding stalls and CAM-table churn, momentarily interrupting traffic for all attached hosts. That matches “all four machines freeze at the same moment and then it all comes back.”
- Localization: Router4 (gateway 192.168.200.1) on interface eth0 is participating in RIP. Evidence: “show run” on router4 includes “router rip … network 192.168.200.0/24” and “show ip rip status” lists eth0 as Send/Recv v2. PCs (pc11–pc14) all use router4 as default gateway and can ARP/ping it normally between events, consistent with intermittent control-plane induced stalls rather than a hard link fault.
- Root cause: Misconfiguration — RIPv2 was mistakenly enabled on the access LAN (192.168.200.0/24) on router4.
- Corrective fix: Remove the LAN from RIP on router4 so no dynamic routing updates are sent to client VLANs. Specifically, under “router rip” on router4, delete “network 192.168.200.0/24” (keep only transit networks 172.25.0.0/16 and 192.168.15.0/24). Optionally enable STP on access switches to harden against control bursts. After this change, client default gateway operation stays local and stable; routing remains dynamic only on router-to-router links.

=== END ===